= Formal language — Technical =

== Definition ==
For alphabet Σ:

<pre>
Σ* = set of all finite strings over Σ
L ⊆ Σ*
</pre>

A language family is a class of such subsets.

== Recognition ==
A machine M recognizes language:

<pre>
L(M) = { w ∈ Σ* : M accepts w }
</pre>

Different machine classes induce different language classes.

== Chomsky-style hierarchy ==
Classical theory distinguishes regular, context-free, context-sensitive, and recursively enumerable languages, associated with different grammar and machine models.

The hierarchy is a useful organizing framework but not the only classification of formal languages.

== Closure and decision properties ==
Language classes are studied through closure under union, intersection, complement, concatenation and other operations, as well as decision problems such as membership, emptiness and equivalence.

